.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A directed graph is a data structure containing two collections of
elements, \fInodes\fR and \fIarcs\fR (also called \fIedges\fR), along
with a relation that connects the nodes and arcs\&.
.PP
Each arc is connected to two nodes, one of which is called the
\fIsource\fR and the other the \fItarget\fR\&. This imposes a
direction upon the arc, which is said to go from the source to the
target\&. When an arc’s source and target are the same
node, the arc is called a \fIloop\fR\&.
.PP
When a node is the source or target of an arc, the node and arc are said
to be \fIadjacent\fR\&. Furthermore, if two nodes are connected via one
or more arcs, the nodes are also said to be \fIadjacent\fR\&.
.PP
A node can be the source of any number of \fIoutgoing arcs\fR, and
also the target of any number of \fIincoming arcs\fR\&. The number of
\fIoutgoing arcs\fR is a node’s \fIout-degree\fR, and the number of
\fIincoming arcs\fR is a node’s \fIin-degree\fR\&.
.PP
This \fBstruct::graph\fR package can manage graphs of nodes, arcs,
and their relationships\&. In addition, the package supports the addition
of any number of named \fIattributes\fR which can be associated with a
graph itself, as well as with any of the graph’s nodes and arcs\&.
.PP
See the \fBstruct::graph::ops\fR package for commands that implement
a wide range of common graph algorithms\&.
.PP
\fINote:\fR The major version of the \fBstruct::graph\fR package
has been changed to version 2, due to backward incompatible changes in
the module’s API\&. Users of version 1 are recommended to read the section
\fBChanges for v2\fR for a full list of the changes, including
incompatibilities with version 1\&.
.PP
As of version 2\&.2, a critcl based C implementation is now provided,
which requires Tcl 8\&.4 or later\&.
.SH API
.TP
\fB::struct::graph\fR ?\fIaGraph\fR? ?\fB=\fR|\fB:=\fR|\fBas\fR|\fBdeserialize\fR \fIsource\fR?
This command creates a new graph object with an associated global Tcl
command\&. If \fIaGraph\fR is given, the global command will be called the
given name; otherwise, a unique name will be generated by the package
itself\&.
.sp
If a \fIsource\fR is specified, the new graph will be initialized from
it\&. For the operators \fB=\fR, \fB:=\fR, and \fBas\fR, the
\fIsource\fR argument is interpreted as the name of another graph
object, and the assignment operator \fB=\fR will be executed\&. For the
operator \fBdeserialize\fR, the \fIsource\fR is assumed to be a
serialized graph object, and \fBdeserialize\fR will be executed\&.
.sp
For example:
.CS


    ::struct::graph graphA = graphB

.CE
.IP
is equivalent to
.CS


    ::struct::graph graphA
    graphA = graphB

.CE
.IP
and
.CS


    ::struct::graph graphA deserialize $graphB

.CE
.IP
is equivalent to
.CS


    ::struct::graph graphA
    graphA deserialize $graphB

.CE
.sp
Once the graph object—and its corresponding command—has been created,
the command can be used to apply various graph operations\&. These all
have the same general form:
.sp
\fIaGraph\fR \fImethod\fR ?\fIarg \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
The \fImethod\fR and the \fIarg\fRs specify what operation to perform\&.
.PP
.SS METHODS
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fB=\fR \fIsourceGraph\fR
The \fB=\fR operator is the graph object \fIassignment\fR operator\&.
It copies the graph contained in the graph object \fIsourceGraph\fR over
the graph data in \fIaGraph\fR\&. The old contents of \fIaGraph\fR are
deleted by this operation\&.
.sp
This operation is in effect equivalent to
.sp
.CS


    \fIaGraph\fR \fBdeserialize\fR [\fIsourceGraph\fR \fBserialize\fR]

.CE
.sp
The operation assumes that the \fIsourceGraph\fR provides a
\fBserialize\fR method which returns a valid graph serialization\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fB-->\fR \fIdestGraph\fR
The \fB-->\fR operator is the graph object \fIreverse assignment\fR
operator\&. It copies the graph contained in the graph object \fIaGraph\fR
over the graph data in the object \fIdestGraph\fR\&.
The old contents of \fIdestGraph\fR are deleted by this operation\&.
.sp
This operation is in effect equivalent to
.sp
.CS


    \fIdestGraph\fR \fBdeserialize\fR [\fIaGraph\fR \fBserialize\fR]

.CE
.sp
The operation assumes that the \fIdestGraph\fR provides a
\fBdeserialize\fR method which takes a graph serialization, and that
\fIaGraph\fR provides a \fBserialize\fR method which returns a valid
graph serialization\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBappend\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the \fIvalue\fR to the graph attribute associated with the given
\fIkey\fR\&. Returns the new value of the graph attribute associated with
the \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBdeserialize\fR \fIserialization\fR
This is the complement of the \fBserialize\fR method\&. It replaces the
graph data in \fIaGraph\fR with the graph described by the
\fIserialization\fR value\&. The old contents of \fIaGraph\fR are deleted
by this operation\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBdestroy\fR
Destroys the graph, including its storage space and associated command\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc append\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the \fIvalue\fR to the given \fIarc\fR’s attribute associated
with the given \fIkey\fR\&. Returns the new value of the \fIarc\fR
attribute associated with the \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc attr\fR \fIkey\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-arcs\fR \fIlist\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-glob\fR \fIglobpattern\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-regexp\fR \fIrepattern\fR
When no options are specified, the \fBarc attr\fR method returns a
\fBdict\fR whose keys are the names of \fIevery\fR arc in the
\fIaGraph\fR graph\&. For each of the resultant dict’s keys, the
corresponding value is the value of the arc attribute associated with
the given \fIkey\fR\&. Arcs which do not have the given \fIkey\fR are not
included in the result \fBdict\fR\&.
.sp
If one of the options is specified, in effect the same results
\fBdict\fR is created as for no options, but filtered so that only the
arcs which match the given option’s constraint are included in the
result \fBdict\fR\&.
.sp
The possible constraints are:
.RS
.TP
\fB-arcs\fR
The value must be a list of arc names\&. Only the arcs mentioned in this list
are searched for the \fIkey\fR attribute\&.
.TP
\fB-glob\fR
The value is a glob pattern\&. Only the arcs in the graph whose names
match this pattern are searched for the \fIkey\fR attribute\&.
.TP
\fB-regexp\fR
The value is a regular expression\&. Only the arcs in the graph whose
names match this pattern are searched for the \fIkey\fR attribute\&.
.RE
.sp
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc delete\fR \fIarc\fR ?\fIarc\fR \&.\&.\&.?
Removes the specified arcs from the graph\&.
.sp
If one or more of the arcs doesn’t exit an error is thrown\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc exists\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the specified \fIarc\fR exists in the graph;
otherwise returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc flip\fR \fIarc\fR
Reverses the direction of the \fIarc\fR, i\&.e\&., the arc’s source and
target nodes are exchanged with each other\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc get\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns the value associated with the \fIarc\fR’s \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
If the \fIarc\fR doesn’t exist or if the \fIkey\fR doesn’t exist, an
error is thrown\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc getall\fR \fIarc\fR ?\fIglob\fR?
Returns a dictionary (suitable for use with [\fBarray set\fR])
of the \fIarc\fR’s attribute keys and values\&.
.sp
If a \fIglob\fR pattern is specified, only the attributes whose keys
match will be included\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc getunweighted\fR
Returns a list of the names of all the graph’s arcs which have \fIno\fR
weight associated with them\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc getweight\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns the weight associated with the \fIarc\fR\&.
.sp
If the \fIarc\fR doesn’t exist or if it has no weight, an error is
thrown\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc keys\fR \fIarc\fR ?\fIglob\fR?
Returns a list of keys for the \fIarc\fR\&.
.sp
If \fIglob\fR is specified only the attributes whose names match the
glob will be part of the returned list\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc keyexists\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the \fIarc\fR has the specified \fIkey\fR;
otherwise returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc insert\fR \fIstart\fR \fIend\fR ?\fIname\fR?
Inserts an arc into the graph beginning at the \fIstart\fR node and
ending at the \fIend\fR node\&. The new arc is called \fIname\fR if
specified; otherwise a unique system-generated name of form
\fIarc\fR\fIx\fR is used\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc lappend\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the given \fIvalue\fR (as a list) to the value associated with
the \fIarc\fR’s \fIkey\fR and returns the \fIarc\fR’s \fIkey\fR’s new
value\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc rename\fR \fIarc\fR \fInewname\fR
Renames the \fIarc\fR to \fInewname\fR and returns \fInewname\fR\&.
.sp
Thows an error if the given \fIarc\fR does not exist, or if an arc
called \fInewname\fR already exists\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc set\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
Get or set the value of the given \fIarc\fR’s associated \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
If \fIvalue\fR \fIis not\fR specified, the value of the given
\fIarc\fR’s associated \fIkey\fR is returned\&. If a \fIvalue\fR
\fIis\fR specified, then the \fIvalue\fR is assigned to the given
\fIkey\fR, and the value of the given \fIarc\fR’s associated \fIkey\fR
is returned\&.
.sp
An arc may have any number of key-value pairs associated with it\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc setunweighted\fR ?\fIweight\fR?
Sets the weight of all arcs without a weight to \fIweight\fR\&. If no
\fIweight\fR is specified it defaults to \fB0\fR\&. Returns the empty
string\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc setweight\fR \fIarc\fR \fIweight\fR
Sets the weight of the \fIarc\fR to \fIweight\fR and returns \fIweight\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc unsetweight\fR \fIarc\fR
Removes the weight of the \fIarc\fR, if present; otherwise does nothing\&.
Returns the empty string\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc hasweight\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the \fIarc\fR has a weight; otherwise
returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc source\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns the node the given \fIarc\fR begins at\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc target\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns the node the given \fIarc\fR ends at\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc nodes\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns a two-element list of the nodes the given \fIarc\fR begins and
ends at\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc move-source\fR \fIarc\fR \fInewsource\fR
Changes the \fIarc\fR’s source node to \fInewsource\fR\&. This can be
described as rotating the arc around its target node\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc move-target\fR \fIarc\fR \fInewtarget\fR
Changes the \fIarc\fR’s target node to \fInewtarget\fR\&. This can be
described as rotating the arc around its source node\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc move\fR \fIarc\fR \fInewsource\fR \fInewtarget\fR
Changes the \fIarc\fR’s source and target nodes to \fInewsource\fR
and \fInewtarget\fR respectively\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc unset\fR \fIarc\fR \fIkey\fR
Removes the \fIarc\fR’s \fIkey\fR and its corresponding value\&.
Does nothing if the \fIkey\fR doesn’t exist\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarc weights\fR
Returns a \fBdict\fR where each key is the name of an arc which has a
weight and each corresponding value is that arc’s weight\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBarcs\fR ?-key \fIkey\fR ?-value \fIvalue\fR?? ?-filter \fIcmdprefix\fR? ?-in|-out|-adj|-inner|-embedding \fInode\fR ?node? \&.\&.\&.?
Returns a list of the graph’s arcs\&.
.sp
If no constraint is specified, every arc is in the returned list\&.
.sp
\fIConstraints\fR
.sp
Constraints can limit which arcs are included in the returned list based
on the nodes that are connected by the arc, on the keyed values
associated with the arc, or both\&. A general filter command can also be
used\&. The constraints that involve connected nodes take a variable
number of nodes as argument, specified after the name of the constraint
itself\&.
.sp
At most one \fB-key\fR constraint may be specified, and if present
then at most one \fB-value\fR constraint my also be used\&. The
\fBkey\fR (and if present \fB-value\fR), if specified, will be
applied \fIsecond\fR\&. (If two or more \fB-key\fR options are used or
if a \fB-value\fR is used without a corresponding \fBkey\fR, an
error will be thrown\&.)
.RS
.TP
\fB-key\fR \fIkey\fR
Only return arcs that have the associated \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-value\fR \fIvalue\fR
Only return arcs that have the associated \fIkey\fR (which \fImust\fR
be specified using the \fB-key\fR option), \fIand\fR for which the
\fIkey\fR’s value is \fIvalue\fR\&.
.RE
.sp
At most one \fB-filter\fR command may be specified, and if present,
this is applied \fIlast\fR\&. (If two or more \fB-filter\fR options
are used an error will be thrown\&.)
.RS
.TP
\fB-filter\fR \fIcmdprefix\fR
Only return arcs for which the given \fIcmdprefix\fR returns \fB1\fR
(true)\&.
.sp
For each candidate arc the \fIcmdprefix\fR is executed in the context of
the caller\&. It is called with two arguments, the name of the graph
object, and the name of the candidate arc\&. The \fIcmdprefix\fR command
should return either \fB1\fR (true; to keep the arc in the list to be
returned), or \fB0\fR (false; to remove the arc from the list to be
returned)\&.
.RE
.sp
At most one constraint may be specified using one of \fB-in\fR,
\fB-out\fR, \fB-adj\fR, \fB-inner\fR, or \fB-embedding\fR\&.
If one of these is present, it is applied \fIfirst\fR\&. (If two or more of
these options is used an error will be thrown\&.)
.RS
.TP
\fB-in\fR
Only return arcs whose target is one of the specified nodes\&. In other
words, compute the union of all the incoming arcs for the specified
nodes\&.
.TP
\fB-out\fR
Only return arcs whose source is one of the specified nodes\&. In other
words, compute the union of all the outgoing arcs for the specified
nodes\&.
.TP
\fB-adj\fR
Only return arcs that are adjacent to at least one of the specified
nodes\&. In other words, compute the union of all the incoming \fIand\fR
outgoing arcs for the specified nodes (i\&.e\&., the union of using
\fB-in\fR and \fB-out\fR)\&.
.TP
\fB-inner\fR
Only return arcs that are adjacent to two of the specified nodes\&. This
is the set of arcs in the subgraph formed by the specified nodes\&.
.TP
\fB-embedding\fR
Only return arcs that are adjacent to exactly one of the specified
nodes\&. This is the set of arcs connecting the subgraph formed by the
specified nodes to the rest of the graph\&.
.RE
.IP
\fINote\fR: After specifiying one of the options above, any word with a
leading dash which is not a valid option is treated as a node name
instead of an invalid option\&. This condition holds until either a valid
option terminates the list of nodes, or the end of the command is
reached, whichever comes first\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBlappend\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the \fIvalue\fR (as a list) to the graph attribute with the
given \fIkey\fR, and returns the attribute with the \fIkey\fR’s new
value\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode append\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the \fIvalue\fR to the \fInode\fR’s attribute with the
given \fIkey\fR, and returns the attribute with the \fIkey\fR’s new
value\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode attr\fR \fIkey\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-nodes\fR \fIlist\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-glob\fR \fIglobpattern\fR
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode attr\fR \fIkey\fR \fB-regexp\fR \fIrepattern\fR
Returns a \fBdict\fR whose keys are node names and whose corresponding
values are those of the nodes’ attribute values associated with the
given \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
The returned \fBdict\fR will include all the graph’s nodes that have
the given \fIkey\fR \fIor\fR if an optional constraint is specified,
the subset of those nodes which match the given constraint\&. This means
that any nodes which do \fInot\fR have the given \fIkey\fR or which do
\fInot\fR satisfy the constraint (if given) will not be included in the
returned \fBdict\fR\&.
.sp
The possible constraints are:
.RS
.TP
\fB-nodes\fR
Only the graph’s nodes that are in the given \fIlist\fR and have the
specified \fIkey\fR, will be included in the results \fBdict\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-glob\fR
Only the graph’s nodes whose names match the given \fIglobpattern\fR
glob pattern and have the specified \fIkey\fR, will be included in the
results \fBdict\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-regexp\fR
Only the graph’s nodes whose names match the given \fIrepattern\fR
regular expression and have the specified \fIkey\fR, will be included in
the results \fBdict\fR\&.
.RE
.sp
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode degree\fR ?-in|-out? \fInode\fR
Returns the number of arcs adjacent to the specified \fInode\fR\&.
.sp
If the \fB-in\fR constraint is specified, only the incoming arcs are
counted; if the \fB-out\fR constraint is specified, only the outgoing
arcs are counted\&. If neither constraint is specified, then all incoming
and outgoing arcs are counted\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode delete\fR \fInode\fR ?\fInode\fR\&.\&.\&.?
Deletes the specified nodes from the graph, \fIand also\fR deletes all
the nodes’ arcs (to prevent unconnected arcs)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode exists\fR \fInode\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the specified \fInode\fR exists in the
graph; otherwise returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode get\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns the value associated with the \fInode\fR’s \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
Throws an error if the \fInode\fR does not have the given \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode getall\fR \fInode\fR ?\fIglob\fR?
Returns a \fBdict\fR (suitable for use with [\fBarray set\fR])
for the \fInode\fR\&. The \fBdict\fR’s keys are attribute names (i\&.e\&.,
their keys) and the values are the corresponding attribute values\&.
.sp
If \fIglob\fR is specified, only the attributes whose names (i\&.e\&., keys)
match this glob pattern, will included in the returned dictionary\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode keys\fR \fInode\fR ?\fIglob\fR?
Returns a list of the \fInode\fR’s attribute keys\&.
.sp
If \fIglob\fR is specified, only the attributes whose names (i\&.e\&., keys)
match this glob pattern, will included in the returned list\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode keyexists\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the \fInode\fR has the specified \fIkey\fR;
otherwise returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode insert\fR ?\fInode\fR\&.\&.\&.?
Inserts one or more nodes into the graph, and returns a list of the
inserted nodes\&. These new nodes are not connected to any arcs\&.
.sp
If no node is specified, a single new node will be inserted and
returned\&. The new node will have a unique name generated by the system
and having the form \fInode\fR\fIx\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode lappend\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Appends the \fIvalue\fR (as a list) the \fInode\fR’s attribute with the
given \fIkey\fR, and returns the attribute with the \fIkey\fR’s new
value\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode opposite\fR \fInode\fR \fIarc\fR
Returns the node at the other end of the specified \fIarc\fR, which must
be adjacent to the given \fInode\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode rename\fR \fInode\fR \fInewname\fR
Renames the \fInode\fR to \fInewname\fR and returns the node’s new name\&.
.sp
An error is thrown if the node does not exist, or if a node called
\fInewname\fR already exists\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode set\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
Get or set the given \fInode\fR’s attribute that’s identified by the
given \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
If \fIvalue\fR is not specified, this command returns the \fIkey\fR’s
current value\&. If \fIvalue\fR is specified, this command assigns
\fIvalue\fR to the \fInode\fR’s \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
Each node may have any number of keyed values associated with it\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnode unset\fR \fInode\fR \fIkey\fR
Removes the \fInode\fR’s \fIkey\fR and its associated value from the
\fInode\fR’s attributes\&. Does nothing if the \fInode\fR does not have
the given \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBnodes\fR ?-key \fIkey\fR? ?-value \fIvalue\fR? ?-filter \fIcmdprefix\fR? ?-in|-out|-adj|-inner|-embedding \fInode\fR \fInode\fR\&.\&.\&.?
Returns a list of the graph’s nodes\&. Every node is included in the list
unless constraints are specified\&.
.sp
Constraints can limit the nodes included in the list based on the keyed
values associated with the node or on neighboring nodes\&. The constraints
that involve neighboring nodes take a list of nodes as argument,
specified after the name of the constraint itself\&.
.sp
The constraints are essentially the same as for the \fBarcs\fR
method\&. Although the exact meanings change slightly, since here the
contraints operate on nodes rather than arcs, the general behaviour is
the same, especially when it comes to the handling of words with a
leading dash in node lists\&.
.sp
\fIConstraints\fR
.RS
.TP
\fB-in\fR
Return the \fIincoming neighbours\fR, i\&.e\&., a list of all the nodes
with at least one outgoing arc ending in a node in the specified set of
nodes\&. (Alternatively specified as the set of source nodes for the node
set’s \fB-in\fR arcs\&.)
.TP
\fB-out\fR
Return the \fIoutgoing neighbours\fR, i\&.e\&., a list of all the nodes
with at least one incoming arc starting in a node in the specified set
of nodes\&. (Alternatively specified as the set of target nodes for the
node set’s \fB-out\fR arcs\&.)
.TP
\fB-adj\fR
Return the \fIneighbours\fR, i\&.e\&., the union of the nodes returned by
\fB-in\fR and \fB-out\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-inner\fR
Return the set of neighbours (see \fB-adj\fR above) which are also in
the given set of nodes, i\&.e\&., the intersection of the neighbours
returned by \fB-adj\fR and the set of nodes\&.
.TP
\fB-embedding\fR
Return the set of neighbours (see \fB-adj\fR above) which are
\fInot\fR in the set of nodes, i\&.e\&., the difference between the
neighbours returned by \fB-adj\fR and the set of nodes\&.
.TP
\fB-key\fR \fIkey\fR
Return only nodes which have the given \fIkey\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-value\fR \fIvalue\fR
Return only nodes which have the given \fIkey\fR and whose associated
value is \fIvalue\fR\&.
.sp
This constraint can only be used in combination with \fB-key\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-filter\fR \fIcmdprefix\fR
Return only those nodes for which the command \fIcmdprefix\fR returns
\fB1\fR (true)\&.
.sp
The command \fIcmdprefix\fR is called with two arguments, the name of
the graph object, and the name of the node in question\&. It is executed
in the context of the caller and must return a Boolean value, \fB0\fR
(false; do not include the node) or \fB1\fR (true; include the node)\&.
.RE
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBget\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns the value associated with the graph’s \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
Throws an error if the graph does not have the given \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
See the \fBset\fR method\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBgetall\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a dictionary (suitable for use with [\fBarray set\fR])
containing all the graph’s keys and their associated values\&.
.sp
If the \fIpattern\fR is specified only those attributes whose names
match the pattern will be part of the returned dictionary\&. The pattern
is a \fBglob\fR pattern\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBkeys\fR ?\fIpattern\fR?
Returns a list of all the graph’s keys\&.
.sp
If the \fIpattern\fR is specified only those attributes whose names
match the pattern will be part of the returned list\&. The pattern is a
\fBglob\fR pattern\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBkeyexists\fR \fIkey\fR
Returns \fB1\fR (true) if the graph has the specified \fIkey\fR;
otherwise returns \fB0\fR (false)\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBserialize\fR ?\fInode\fR\&.\&.\&.?
Returns the graph (or a subgraph of the graph) serialized into a single
Tcl value\&. The value can be deserialized back into a graph using the
\fBdeserialize\fR method\&.
.sp
The serialization is of the entire graph, unless one or more \fInode\fRs
are specified, in which case only the subgraph spanned by the given
nodes is serialized\&.
.sp
Since a serialized graph is a Tcl value it can be copied like any Tcl
string, e\&.g\&., sent to a channel, persisted into a file, etc\&.
.sp
This method also serves as the basis for the graph’s copy constructor and
assignment operator\&.
.sp
Every graph interface’s serialize method’s implementation must produce
semantically equivalent serialization values so that these values may be
used interchangeably between implementations\&.
.sp
The serialized value is a list of triples, plus a \fBdict\fR at the
end\&. This means that \fI[llength $serial] % 3 == 1\fR\&. Valid values
include 1, 4, 7, …\&.
.sp
The last element of the list is a \fBdict\fR containing the attributes
associated with the whole graph\&.
.sp
All the preceding elements are triples, one per node, each consisting of:
.RS
.IP [1]
The node’s name\&.
.IP [2]
A \fBdict\fR containing the node’s attributes\&.
.IP [3]
A list of all the arcs starting at this node (see below)\&.
.RE
.sp
Each element of a node’s list of arcs is itself a list of three or four
elements as follows:
.RS
.IP [1]
The arc’s name\&.
.IP [2]
A reference to the arc’s destination node\&. This reference is the integer
index of the destination node in the main serialization list\&. (This
integer will always be \fI>= 0\fR and
\fI< [llength $serial]\fR and a multiple of three\&.)
.sp
\fINote:\fR For internal consistency serialization requires that every
arc name used in a graph must be unique, whether in the same node, or at
some other node\&.
.IP [3]
A \fBdict\fR containing the arc’s attributes\&.
.IP [4]
An optional arc weight\&. If absent it means that this arc has no
associated weight\&.
.sp
\fINote:\fR This information is new, compared to the serialization of
\fBgraph\fR 2\&.3 and earlier\&. Making arc weights optional in the new
format preserves some compatibility with the old\&. In particular, any
graph that does \fInot\fR use weights will have a serialization which
is deserializeable by the older graph package\&. However, if weights are
used, older packages will not be able to deserialize\&.
.RE
.sp
For all attribute dictionaries, each key is an attribute name and each
key’s associated value holds the attribute’s value\&.
.sp
\fINote:\fR The serialization’s order of nodes and of arcs within each
node has no significance\&. In other words two semantically equivalent
graphs could have different serializations\&.
.CS


    # A possible serialization for the graph structure
    #
    #        d -----> %2
    #       /         ^ \\
    #      /         /   \\
    #     /         b     \\
    #    /         /       \\
    #  %1 <- a - %0         e
    #    ^         \\\\      /
    #     \\\\        c     /
    #      \\\\        \\\\  /
    #       \\\\        v v
    #        f ------ %3
    # is
    #
    # %3 {} {{f 6 {}}} %0 {} {{a 6 {}} {b 9 {}} {c 0 {}}} %1 {} {{d 9 {}}} %2 {} {{e 0 {}}} {}
    #
    # This assumes that the graph has neither attribute data nor weighted arcs\&.

.CE
.sp
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBset\fR \fIkey\fR ?\fIvalue\fR?
Get or set the value associated with the given graph \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
If no \fIvalue\fR is given the value associated with the \fIkey\fR is
returned\&. If \fIvalue\fR is given, the \fIkey\fR is set to the specified
\fIvalue\fR and the \fIvalue\fR is returned\&.
.sp
Throws an error if no \fIvalue\fR is specified (i\&.e\&., when getting) and
the graph does not have the given \fIkey\fR\&.
.sp
A graph may have any number of keyed values associated with it\&. See also
the \fBget\fR method\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBswap\fR \fInode1\fR \fInode2\fR
Swap the positions of \fInode1\fR and \fInode2\fR in the graph\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBunset\fR \fIkey\fR
Removes the graph’s \fIkey\fR and its corresponding value\&.
Does nothing if the \fIkey\fR doesn’t exist\&.
.TP
\fIaGraph\fR \fBwalk\fR \fInode\fR ?-type \fItype\fR? ?-order \fIorder\fR? ?-dir \fIdirection\fR? -command \fIcmd\fR
Perform a breadth-first or depth-first walk of the graph starting at
\fInode\fR and going \fBforward\fR (the direction of outgoing arcs) or
\fBbackward\fR (the opposite direction to the incoming arcs)\&.
.sp
If specified, the walk’s type must be \fBdfs\fR (depth-first; the
default), or \fBbfs\fR breadth-first\&.
.sp
If specified, the walk’s order must be \fBpre\fR (pre-order; the
default), or \fBpost\fR (post-order), or \fBboth\fR\&.
.sp
Pre-order means that a node is visited before any of its neighbours (as
defined by the \fIdirection\fR, see below)\&. Post-order means that a
node is visited after any of its neighbours\&. Both-order walking means
that a node is visited before \fIand\fR after any of its neighbours\&. Note
that the breadth-first type of walk may \fIonly\fR be combined with
pre-order; only depth-first may be used with pre-order, post-order, or
both-order\&.
.sp
If specified, the walk’s direction must be \fBbackward\fR (the
direction opposite to the incoming arcs), or \fBforward\fR (the
direction of the outgoing arcs)\&.
.sp
As the walk progresses, the command \fIcmd\fR will be evaluated at each
node and passed three arguments\&. The arguments are the call’s mode
(\fBenter\fR or \fBleave\fR), the graph itself (i\&.e\&., \fIaGraph\fR),
and the current node’s name\&. For a pre-order walk, all nodes are entered
(mode \fBenter\fR is passed to \fIcmd\fR); for a post-order all nodes
are left (mode \fBleave\fR is passed)\&. In a both-order walk the first
visit to a node is the entry (mode \fBenter\fR), and the second visit
is the leave (mode \fBleave\fR)\&.
.PP
.SH "CHANGES FOR V2"
.SS "CHANGES FOR V2\&.4"
.PP
The \fBserialize\fR method’s serialization format changed to account
for the (optional) use of weights\&. See the method’s documentation for
details\&.
.SS "CHANGES FOR V2\&.X"
The following noteworthy changes have occurred:
.IP [1]
The API for accessing attributes and their values has been
simplified\&.
.sp
All functionality regarding the default attribute "data" has been
removed\&. This default attribute no longer exists\&. All accesses to
attributes must specify the attribute’s name\&. This backward
\fIincompatible\fR change led to simplified signatures for all methods
involving attributes\&.
.sp
In particular, the flag \fB-key\fR has gone\&. Please read the
documentation for the arc and node methods \fBget\fR,
\fBgetall\fR, \fBset\fR, \fBunset\fR, \fBappend\fR,
\fBlappend\fR, \fBkeyexists\fR, and \fBkeys\fR, for a
description of the new APIs\&.
.IP [2]
The methods \fBkeys\fR and \fBgetall\fR now take an optional
glob pattern argument which if specified will limit the returned
attribute data to only those keys which match the pattern\&.
.IP [3]
Arcs and nodes can now be renamed\&. See the documentation for the
methods \fBarc rename\fR and \fBnode rename\fR\&.
.IP [4]
The structure has been extended with APIs for graph serialization and
deserialization, and a number of operations based on these methods
(e\&.g\&., graph assignment, copy construction)\&.
.sp
Please read the documentation for the methods \fBserialize\fR,
\fBdeserialize\fR, \fB=\fR, and \fB-->\fR, and the
documentation on the construction of graph objects\&.
.sp
Serialization and deserialization mean that graphs can be copied like
any Tcl string, e\&.g\&., sent to a channel, persisted into a file, etc\&.
.IP [5]
Both \fBarc\fR’s and \fBnode\fR’s now have an \fBattr\fR method
which supports access to attribute data regardless of arc and node
relationships\&.
.IP [6]
Both \fBarcs\fR and \fBnodes\fR have been extended with
the ability to specify constraints to filter the arcs or nodes they
return; see their documentation for details\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example shows the basics of using the
\fBstruct::graph\fR package\&.
.PP
An illustration of the graph used in the example:
.CS


                SW45
          /------------------BOS
         /                   /|
        /  /--→ORD      NW35/ |
       ↙  /   / D↑  AA1387 ↙  |
    SFO  /  U/  L| /-----JFK  |
        /   A|  3| |      |   |
       |    8|  3| |      |   |
  UA120|    7|  5/ /      |   |
       |    7↓  / /       /   |DL247
       |     DFW←/      A/    |
       |    /   ↖       A|    |
       |   /AA49 \\      9|   /
      LAX←/       \\     0|  /
        ↖     AA523\\    3↓ ↙
         \\          \\--MIA
          \\-----------/
            AA411

.CE
.PP
The code that represents the graph shown above, along with weights
(flight distances in kilometers)\&.
.CS


proc visit_airport {mode graph airport} {
    puts " [expr {$mode eq "enter" ? "→" : "←"}] $graph $airport"
}

try { ;# Adapted from "Data Structures and Algorithms in Java"
    ::struct::graph flights
    flights node insert SFO
    flights node insert LAX
    flights node insert ORD
    flights node insert DFW
    flights node insert JFK
    flights node insert BOS
    flights node insert MIA
    flights arc insert JFK SFO SW45
    flights arc setweight SW45 4152
    flights arc insert JFK DFW AA1387
    flights arc setweight AA1387 2235
    flights arc insert JFK MIA AA903
    flights arc setweight AA903 1756
    flights arc insert BOS JFK NW35
    flights arc setweight NW35 300
    flights arc insert BOS MIA DL247
    flights arc setweight DL247 2027
    flights arc insert MIA DFW AA523
    flights arc setweight AA523 1802
    flights arc insert MIA LAX AA411
    flights arc setweight AA411 3763
    flights arc insert LAX ORD UA120
    flights arc setweight UA120 2802
    flights arc insert DFW LAX AA49
    flights arc setweight AA49 1983
    flights arc insert DFW ORD DL335
    flights arc setweight DL335 1290
    flights arc insert ORD DFW UA877
    flights arc setweight UA877 1290
    puts "flights walk ORD:"
    puts [flights walk ORD -order both -command visit_airport]
    puts "All flights: [lsort -dictionary [flights arcs]]"
    puts "Chicago flights: [flights arcs -embedding ORD]"
    puts "Chicago direct connections: [flights nodes -embedding ORD]"
} finally {
    flights destroy
}
=>
flights walk ORD:
 → flights ORD
 → flights DFW
 → flights LAX
 ← flights LAX
 ← flights DFW
 ← flights ORD

All flights: AA49 AA411 AA523 AA903 AA1387 DL247 DL335 NW35 SW45 UA120 UA877
Chicago flights: DL335 UA120 UA877
Chicago direct connections: DFW LAX

.CE
.SH "BUGS, IDEAS, FEEDBACK"
If you find errors in this document or bugs or problems with the
package it describes, or if you want to suggest improvements for the
documentation or the package, please use the
\fITcllib Trackers\fR [http://core\&.tcl\&.tk/tcllib/reportlist] and specify
\fIstruct :: graph\fR as the category\&.
.PP
When proposing code changes, please provide \fIunified diffs\fR,
i\&.e the output of \fBdiff -u\fR\&.
.PP
Note further that \fIattachments\fR are strongly preferred over
inlined patches\&. Attachments can be made by going to the \fBEdit\fR
form of the ticket immediately after its creation, and then using the
left-most button in the secondary navigation bar\&.
